  2	//!
  3	//! This is implemented as a bit set. There is a preallocation segment that
  4	//! is used for almost all screen sizes. Then there is a dynamically allocated
  5	//! segment if the screen is larger than the preallocation amount.
  6	//!
  7	//! In reality, tabstops don't need to be the most performant in any metric.
  8	//! This implementation tries to balance denser memory usage (by using a bitset)
  9	//! and minimizing unnecessary allocations.
